; calcium silicate hydrate matrix + aggregate (sand, gravel, crushed stone).
; made by mixing: cement (CaO + SiO2, calcined) + water + aggregate.
; hydration reaction: CaO + SiO2 + H2O → calcium silicate hydrate gel.
; the gel fills pores and binds aggregate → stone-like solid.
; 
; properties:
; compressive strength: ~30-50 MPa (normal). ~100 MPa (high strength).
; tensile strength: very low (~3 MPa) — concrete cracks under tension.
; solution: steel reinforcement (rebar) carries the tensile load.
; reinforced concrete: the dominant structural material of the modern world.
; 
; the chemistry: C-S-H (calcium silicate hydrate) is a poorly-ordered polymer.
; it grows as needles that interlock and fill space — micro-scale composite.
; water/cement ratio determines strength: lower w/c → stronger (fewer pores).
; 
; CO2 footprint: cement production = ~8% of global CO2 emissions.
; CaCO3 → CaO + CO2 at 900°C — each tonne of cement releases ~0.7 tonne CO2.

